WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.357
Client-side validation is valuable for usability.

00:00:02.357 --> 00:00:05.366
It can catch missing fields, obvious formatting errors,

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:08.751
and range problems before a user submits a form.

00:00:08.751 --> 00:00:10.255
Browser controls, frontend libraries,

00:00:10.255 --> 00:00:15.521
and shared schemas can make interfaces feel responsive and reduce unnecessary server round trips.

00:00:15.521 --> 00:00:19.282
But client-side validation is not a security control by itself.

00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:22.667
Browsers and clients can be modified, bypassed, or replaced.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:27.180
A user can send requests directly to an API, change hidden fields,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:32.446
alter JavaScript in the browser, replay a request, or build a different client entirely.

00:00:32.446 --> 00:00:37.335
For that reason, server-side validation must enforce the real rules before processing, storing,

00:00:37.335 --> 00:00:39.216
or acting on untrusted data.

00:00:39.216 --> 00:00:41.849
Shared schemas can reduce duplicated inconsistent rules.

00:00:41.849 --> 00:00:45.986
A team may use the same schema definition, or generated models,

00:00:45.986 --> 00:00:47.866
across frontend and backend code.

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:53.132
This can improve consistency, but it does not eliminate the need for server enforcement.

00:00:53.132 --> 00:00:58.398
The server should assume that any client-side check may have been skipped or altered.

00:00:58.398 --> 00:01:00.654
Error messages should be designed carefully.

00:01:00.654 --> 00:01:05.168
They should help legitimate users fix input problems without exposing stack traces,

00:01:05.168 --> 00:01:08.177
database structure, secret values, or unnecessary implementation details.

00:01:08.177 --> 00:01:10.433
The same principle applies to logs:

00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:13.818
record enough information to debug and monitor invalid input,

00:01:13.818 --> 00:01:19.460
but avoid storing raw sensitive values or payloads that could make logs risky to handle.
